Each generator expression visits all the coordinates in turn, and built-in min and max work just as well on floats as they do on integers, so the function handles real points without extra code. When there is only one point, as in the second example, it is equal to both its own minimum and maximum in each axis, so the returned tuple collapses to four equal values and is correct.

The examples in the prompt are [(0, 0), (2, 3), (1, 1)], [(5, 5)] and [(-1, -1), (1, 1)], and a placing call I traced separately is bounding_box([(1.2, -0.5), (0, 3.7)]) which should give (0, -0.5, 1.2, 3.7). Each of those is a single fourfold computation, so the implementation is the same for any collection of points.

