As noted in the question, three distinct points lie on a single straight line if the area of the triangle they form is zero. A handy way to compute that area in coordinates is the determinant:

        (x₂ − x₁)(y₃ − y₁) − (x₃ − x₁)(y₂ − y₁)  =  2 × (area of triangle)

This is twice the area, so the sign is the direction of the turn, and the value equals zero exactly when the three points are collinear. Because it only uses pairwise differences, the formula applies even when one or two of the points coincide.

