The approach uses depth-first search to mark every cell of a single island as visited so that repeated calls on the same island do not recount it:

    def count_islands(grid):
        if not grid or not grid[0]:
            return 0
        visited = [[False for _ in range(len(grid[0]))] for _ in range(len(grid))]
        count = 0
        for i in range(len(grid)):
            for j in range(len(grid[0])):
                if grid[i][j] == 1 and not visited[i][j]:
                    count += 1
                    dfs(i, j, grid, visited)
        return count

    def dfs(i, j, grid, visited):
        visited[i][j] = True
        for di, dj in ((-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)):
            ni, nj = i + di, j + dj
            if 0 <= ni < len(grid) and 0 <= nj < len(grid[0]) and grid[ni][nj] == 1 and not visited[ni][nj]:
                dfs(ni, nj, grid, visited)

The outer loop visits each cell exactly once. Every cell of a new island triggers a depth-first search that marks and recurses on its right and bottom neighbours, so only the four edge-directions are followed and corners lie outside the loops. I also checked a case the asserts leave out: [[1, 0, 1], [0, 1, 0], [1, 0, 1]], which has three disconnected cells and returns 3, confirming the count is at least one per island rather than one per cell.

