                acct, amt = event[1], event[2]
                balances[acct] = balances.get(acct, 0) + amt
            elif event[0] == 'withdraw':
                acct, amt = event[1], event[2]
                if balances.get(acct, 0) >= amt:
                    balances[acct] = balances[acct] - amt
            elif event[0] == 'transfer':
                from_acct, to_acct, amt = event[1], event[2], event[3]
                if balances.get(from_acct, 0) >= amt:
                    balances[from_acct] = balances[from_acct] - amt
                    balances[to_acct] = balances.get(to_acct, 0) + amt
        return dict(balances)

The deposit branch adds to an existing account or creates it at 0. The withdrawal compares the current balance against the amount and subtracts only when sufficient. The transfer first asks whether the source has enough; when it does, the amount is taken out of the source and added to the destination, which may also require creation. Because a failed withdrawal or transfer does nothing, including to a destination, both sides of such a transfer stay as they were.

