The string has to be split like this: everything before "://" is the scheme; everything after "://", up to and including the next slash or question mark, is the host; from the first slash comes the path, stopping before any question mark; and from the first question mark comes the query, or an empty string if none.

A regular expression is a natural fit. The relevant pattern is

    ^([a-zA-Z0-9+.-]+)://([^/?#]+)(/[^?#]*)?(?:(\?[^\x00]*)|)$

The first capture group is the scheme, the second group is the host, the third picks up the path only if it comes immediately after the slashes, and the fourth is the query, which we take only when it appears; the eighth slot holds the query when it does. The look-ahead for the path excludes any slash that occurs inside the host part, so the path really starts at the first slash after the placing rule. Any fragment is ignored with .+ that carry on beyond double hashes.

