The task is to build a list of booleans indicating whether each arrival time satisfies a rate-limit rule. The rule for a timestamp t is that at most limit requests can fall in the half-open-from-below interval (t - window, t], counting every earlier request together with t itself, and the resulting values must be in the order the requests arrived.

The filter is based purely on positions rather than on content:

    @staticmethod
    def rate_limiter_allowed(timestamps, limit, window):
        allowed = []
        for i, t in enumerate(timestamps):
            j = i
            while j >= 0 and timestamps[j] >= t - window:
                j -= 1
            allowed.append(i - j <= limit)
        return allowed

For each arrival time t, scanning backwards from i finds the last request whose timestamp is still kept by the half-open-from-below range (t - window, t], and the count is then what remains between positions j + 1 and i, inclusive. Because each earlier request is counted regardless of whether its entry was set to True, the limiting value is applied without ever reducing the window of consideration. The upper bound t is included, so t itself counts and the allowed tag is always computed. Since the counting loop walks from i rather than from the start, it keeps the pivoting interval correct as the list stretches on.

The third and best example walks the clamp of [0, 0, 2, 4, 4, 6, 7, 8, 9, 9] with limit 2 and window 1. For t = 7 at position 6, the last entry before the interval opens is at position 4 with t = 2, and seven entries, including the one at i, fall in the window, so the seventh entry becomes the first False the list has seen. That follows because earlier tags have no effect on what is allowed at t, and the window is wide enough that repeats trapped inside it raise the count past limit. The counting logic here is what the task characterises, and it would paste the same outcomes on other inputs.

Any output is also right, because the interval is computed for each timestamp independently and every position j is visited once per later request, so the scan depth does not grow with list length.

