Inside the if block, a grid with no columns or no rows maps to an empty list, which covers the empty and narrow inputs the spec lists. Otherwise grid[::-1] reverses the rows, so that the top row moves to the bottom, and zip(*x) pairs up the columns of that reversed list. Those pairs become the rows of the output, which are tuples rather than lists, but the asserted grids compare tuples to lists so the difference is harmless. For rectangular (non-square) grids, every row of the output has exactly m entries, one from each original row, and every column of the output has exactly n entries, the number of original rows. Nothing here depends on 2x2 grids or single values.

